  "name": "Question 12",
  "problem": "Expressed in **ascending order** in radians, the exact solutions to the equation $2\\operatorname{cot}⁡x\\operatorname{sec}⁡x+\\operatorname{cot}⁡x-2\\operatorname{sec}⁡x-1=0,$ for $-\\pi \\le x<\\pi ,$ are\n\n${x}_{1}=$\n{{field-1}}$,\\,{x}_{2}=$ \n{{field-2}}.",
  "worked_solution": "Note that the functions $\\operatorname{cot}⁡x$ and $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x$ are not defined at the points where $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=0$ or $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=0.$ On the interval $-\\pi \\le x<\\pi ,$ the excluded values are $x=0,\\,\\pm \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,\\pm \\pi .$\n\nFirst, we factor the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{cot}⁡x\\operatorname{sec}⁡x+\\operatorname{cot}⁡x-2\\operatorname{sec}⁡x-1 & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{cot}⁡x(2\\operatorname{sec}⁡x+1)-(2\\operatorname{sec}⁡x+1) & =0 \\\\ (2\\operatorname{sec}⁡x+1)(\\operatorname{cot}⁡x-1) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the zero-product property, we have to solve two equations: \n\n$$\n2\\operatorname{sec}⁡x+1=0,\\,\\operatorname{cot}⁡x-1=0\n$$\n\nLet's solve each equation separately.\n\n- First, we solve $2\\operatorname{sec}⁡x+1=0,$ or equivalently $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=-2.$ But since $|\\operatorname{cos}⁡x|\\le 1,$ then $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=-2$ has no solution.\n\n- Then, we solve $\\operatorname{cot}⁡x-1=0,$ or equivalently $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=1.$ The solutions to this equation for $-\\pi \\le x<\\pi ,$ are $x=-\\frac{3\\pi }{4},\\,\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$\n\nArranging all solutions from both equations in ascending order, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=-\\frac{3\\pi }{4},\\,{x}_{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\n$$",
